questions.q:
Two relations are defined on the set A = {1, 2, 3}:  
R₁ = {(1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3)}  
R₂ = {(1, 2), (2, 3), (1, 3)}  
Compare R₁ and R₂ in terms of: (a) whether they are subsets of A × A, (b) number of elements, (c) domain and range. What does this tell you about the variety of possible relations on a set?

questions.a:
(a) Both are subsets of A × A (since all ordered pairs have elements from A).  
(b) n(R₁) = 3, n(R₂) = 3 — same cardinality but different elements.  
(c) R₁: Domain = {1,2,3}, Range = {1,2,3}  
R₂: Domain = {1,2}, Range = {2,3}  
**Insight:** Relations on the same set can have identical size but completely different structure — one is diagonal (identity-like), the other is a chain. This shows relations are **not** characterized by size alone; the *pattern* of pairing matters.  
**Wrong answer reveals:** If the student says R₂ is not a relation on A because (3,?) is missing, they think a relation must include all elements of A in the domain. They confuse "relation on A" (subset of A×A) with "function on A" (domain = A). This is a critical Class 11 distinction.
